---
import Shot from './Shot.astro';
import { docs, withBase } from '../data/content';

const sizes = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 690px';
const miniSizes = '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 360px';
---
<section id="weather" aria-labelledby="weather-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Weather</span>
      <h2 id="weather-title">The weather, on the chart you're using.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Forecast grids, tropical cyclone advisories and surface analyses draw over your charts, on the same timeline as
        tides and currents, and Check route reads them at your ETA. Weather data isn't S-100 data and isn't for
        navigation: use official forecasts and warnings to plan and make a passage.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Wind and waves</span>
          <h3>Wind and waves over the chart.</h3>
          <p>
            Open GRIB2 and NetCDF forecasts, such as NOAA's GFS and GFS-Wave, and see wave height, wind barbs and
            isobars. Step through the run on the Timeline. Marine warnings, buoy reports and your own wind instruments
            join them.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>GRIB2 (including JPEG 2000), NetCDF-CF and CAP warnings</li>
            <li>Isobars, a Beaufort wind fill and wave arrows in Display controls</li>
            <li>NDBC buoys and own-ship wind from NMEA 0183</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.weather)}>Weather guide</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W1"
          alt="Hurricane Erin off Cape Hatteras: NOAA GFS-Wave wave height and GFS wind barbs and isobars over the NOAA charts"
          title="Wind and waves"
          detail="Hurricane Erin, 21 Aug 2025: GFS-Wave wave height, GFS 10 m wind barbs and isobars over NOAA charts off Cape Hatteras, Display controls open"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Ocean</span>
          <h3>Sea temperature and currents.</h3>
          <p>
            Sea surface temperature fills in a band for each degree, so fronts such as the edge of the Gulf Stream
            show as closely spaced bands, with isotherms every 2 °C if you want them. Ocean currents draw in S-111's
            speed bands and colours, so a model current reads the same as an S-111 dataset.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Daily MUR, OISST and Geo-polar Blended analyses from NOAA ERDDAP</li>
            <li>Currents from satellite altimetry and US coastal HF radar</li>
            <li>HYCOM and Copernicus Marine files open too</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.weatherShow)}>Show a forecast</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W2"
          alt="The Gulf Stream in the Florida Straits: NASA JPL MUR sea surface temperature with isotherms and NOAA AOML current arrows over the NOAA charts"
          title="Sea temperature and currents"
          detail="Florida Straits, 10 Feb 2025: MUR SST fill + isotherms, AOML altimetry currents, NOAA charts"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Hazards</span>
          <h3>Ice, fog, rain and thunderstorms.</h3>
          <p>
            Freezing spray is worked out from the wind, air temperature and sea temperature with the icing predictor
            NOAA's Ocean Prediction Center uses, and drawn in four classes from light to extreme. Restricted visibility
            uses the bands of a marine forecast, and nothing is drawn where it's good, so the chart stays clear.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Freezing spray, with dashed air temperature isotherms</li>
            <li>Very poor, poor and moderate visibility</li>
            <li>Precipitation rate, thunderstorm risk from CAPE, and cloud cover</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.weatherShow)}>Show a forecast</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W3"
          alt="Freezing spray round Kodiak Island and Shelikof Strait in an Arctic outbreak, with air temperature isotherms and isobars from NOAA GFS over the NOAA charts"
          title="Freezing spray"
          detail="Kodiak, Alaska, 15 Dec 2025: GFS freezing spray, air isotherms, isobars, NOAA charts"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Tropical cyclones</span>
          <h3>The storm, where it will be.</h3>
          <p>
            Add the storms NHC is issuing advisories for from the Library, or open an advisory's files. SoundCharts draws
            the cone, forecast track, wind radii and past track, and the storm at the view time, estimated between
            forecast points, so stepping through the Timeline moves it along its track.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>34, 50 and 64 kt wind radii</li>
            <li>Marked stale once a newer advisory is likely, and final when NHC stops issuing</li>
            <li>Atlantic and East and Central Pacific storms</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.weatherCyclones)}>Show tropical cyclones</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W4"
          alt="Hurricane Erin passing north of Puerto Rico and the Virgin Islands: the NHC cone, forecast track, wind radii and the storm at the view time, with the wind radii over the NOAA charts"
          title="Tropical cyclones"
          detail="Hurricane Erin, NHC advisory 22 (16 Aug 2025 21Z): cone, track, radii, storm at view time, NOAA charts"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="feature">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">Surface analysis</span>
          <h3>Fronts and pressure centres.</h3>
          <p>
            The Weather Prediction Center's coded surface analysis draws like a printed weather map: cold, warm, occluded
            and stationary fronts, troughs, and highs and lows with their central pressure. The Library fetches each new
            analysis as WPC issues it, every 3 hours, and an analysis fades once it's 6 hours old.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The analysis and WPC's forecast fronts, each at its own time</li>
            <li>North America and the waters around it, WPC's area</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.weatherFronts)}>Show a surface analysis</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W5"
          alt="WPC surface analysis off the Carolinas: a low and its cold front with GFS isobars over the NOAA charts"
          title="Surface analysis"
          detail="WPC high-resolution analysis, 28 Oct 2025 00Z, with GFS isobars, Southeast NOAA charts"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="feature flip">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <span class="eyebrow">On a route</span>
          <h3>Weather at your ETA.</h3>
          <p>
            Set a wind or wave limit and Check route reads the loaded forecast at the time you'd reach each point. It
            lists the stretches above a limit, with their time window and highest value, and the stretches the forecast
            doesn't reach. With an NHC advisory loaded, it also tests the route against the storm where it will be at
            each ETA. The findings are for planning, not navigation.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>10 m wind and significant wave height</li>
            <li>The storm's 34, 50 and 64 kt wind radii, its cone and your closest approach, with Must review inside the 50 kt radius</li>
            <li>The departures that keep the route within your limits and out of the storm</li>
            <li>The same check in <code>s100 route check</code></li>
          </ul>
          <a class="more" href={withBase(docs.routeWeather)}>Weather along the route</a>
        </div>
        <Shot
          id="W6"
          alt="Check route from Charleston to the Chesapeake Bay entrance as Hurricane Erin passed offshore: the stretch off Cape Hatteras inside Erin's 34 kt wind radius, and wind and wave findings above the limits"
          title="Weather on a route"
          detail="Charleston → Chesapeake, departing 20 Aug 2025 12Z: GFS wind + GFS-Wave, NHC advisory 36, 25 kn / 3.5 m limits, Weather findings"
          sizes={sizes}
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="minis three">
      <div class="mini">
        <Shot
          id="W8"
          alt="Sea fog off eastern Maine: NOAA GFS restricted visibility over the NOAA charts of Penobscot Bay and Mount Desert"
          title="Restricted visibility"
          detail="Gulf of Maine, 20 Jun 2025: GFS visibility bands"
          sizes={miniSizes}
        />
        <div class="body">
          <h3>Fog</h3>
          <p>Visibility under 5 nautical miles in three greys, from moderate to fog.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <Shot
          id="W9"
          alt="Rain and thunderstorm risk over south Florida from NOAA GFS, over the NOAA charts"
          title="Rain and thunderstorms"
          detail="Florida, 10 Aug 2025: GFS precipitation and CAPE"
          sizes={miniSizes}
        />
        <div class="body">
          <h3>Rain and thunderstorms</h3>
          <p>Precipitation in rain radar colours, thunderstorm risk where CAPE is high, and a light cloud veil.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <Shot
          id="W7"
          alt="The Library listing its weather sources: GFS, sea surface temperature from ERDDAP, NHC's active storms and WPC's surface analysis"
          title="Weather sources"
          detail="Library: GFS, ERDDAP SST, NHC storms, WPC analysis"
          sizes={miniSizes}
        />
        <div class="body">
          <h3>Sources in the Library</h3>
          <p>
            GFS runs, ERDDAP analyses, NHC storms and WPC analyses for the map view, refreshed as new ones are issued and
            tagged Stale when they're old.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
